WEBVTT

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:02.810
The second match of the Monaco Cup finals.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.940
Due to repeated sabotage, the
match at the deserted island was nullified;

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:10.240
the rematch was to take place at
the San Agustin River in Mexico.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.490
But, the Japanese representatives were

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.630
dropped into the Huautla Cave,
which has a depth of 1500m.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.040
Lost within the caves,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.540
the Japanese representatives and Pierrot

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.630
came upon the Iglesia Falls, which
connected to a downstream river.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:30.510
However, the river In the cave was more
like a flooded underground water tunnel.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.640
Without diving equipment to breathe underwater,

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.150
it was impossible to escape.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.600
Once you’re a world-level clown...

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.270
You can hold your breath and swim for 2-3km.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.270
So, for now, I will swim down the river and call for help.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.730
Th... Th... The amazing world-level!

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.620
But, I’m really hungry right now.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.660
No need to worry.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.410
I have all of the ingredients ready.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:07.050
How about making the bread for
the match using the blind cave tetra?

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.510
If you make me a delicious bread...

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.550
I think I will get back my stamina and be able to swim.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:14.760
I see!

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.550
In that case, leave it to us!

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:17.600
Yeah.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.470
Let’s make it!

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:20.220
All right!

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.360
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.280
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.570
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.950
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:42.160
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.720
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.590
Pierrot’s big miscalculation!

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.150
The Last Supper. How does it taste?

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.350
All right, let’s do this!

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:31.190
Let’s make a super-delicious bread to
give Pierrot-han the stamina he needs.

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:31.860
Okay.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.730
Yeah, let’s do this!

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.860
Hey, Mr. Clown.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.280
Huh? Wh-What is it?

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.120
Do you have any fishing rods?

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.870
The fish here are so small that even my sword, Kaijinmaru, cannot catch them.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.160
Of course I do.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.590
After all, the plan was to make bread with fish caught from the river...

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.760
Here you go.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:00.140
Hey! Why would we need so many when there’s only three of us?!

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.720
Oh, that’s right.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:06.220
Is something the matter? You look rather pale.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.570
What are you saying?

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.900
Clowns are always pale.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.030
That’s true...

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.030
First, let’s make some bait from the flour.

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:17.860
All right!

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.280
I’m going to catch me some big ones!

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.410
I’m sorry, Japanese representatives.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.410
It was impossible from the very beginning

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.790
for me to help us escape from these caves

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:30.670
by swimming 2-3km while holding my breath...

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.220
Because I’m...

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.890
a deadweight in water.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.810
Man, that’s a big haul.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:50.280
They’re biting at every cast.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.440
Well, there usually isn’t anybody
fishing for them here in the caves,

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.520
so they aren’t wary of being caught.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.780
Hey, how about joining us, Old... Mister Pierrot?

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:01.820
This is a lot of fun.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.660
Uh, no no no no.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.030
I’ll pass.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.540
I have to conserve my strength, after all.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.370
Yeah, Azuma.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:12.040
Don’t cause trouble for our Great Savior.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:12.960
Yeah.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.040
That’s true...

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.040
This is bad.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.010
They are catching the fish easily.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.470
They’ll make the bread at this rate!

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:27.390
I must think of another way to escape quickly...

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:31.560
Or I really will have to swim.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:36.700
How is it, Kawachi?

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:40.030
They don’t seem poisonous,

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:41.860
but they’re really disgusting.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.580
It feels like I’m eating goldfish.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:46.280
Disgusting!

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.620
They stink, and their meat’s dry.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.170
They ain’t fit for consumption.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:54.790
Mr. Clown.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:57.380
We fished them, but they don’t taste too good.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.460
We have flour and seasoning,

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:03.680
so I think that we should give up on making bread with fish, and simply make regular bread.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:04.090
What do you think?

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.300
No! No, no, no, no!

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.550
You can make a regular bread really quickly, right?

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:10.100
But, why?

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:14.060
Why must we prepare a bread with fish for the match when we’re in such a crisis?!

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:16.520
There’s a very good reason for it.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.440
I didn’t mention this before,

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.780
but my favorite food is fish.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:21.690
No...

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.520
Actually, I think I prefer meat.

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.240
Since this may be my last supper,

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.670
I really want to eat a delicious bread made with fish.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.030
The truth is that I just want to buy some more time...

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.960
The last supper...? You can’t be serious.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.670
Pierrot-han... you plan on dying?

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.920
If that should happen, we’ll die, too!

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.020
I’m not planning on dying, but...

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.850
Even a world-level clown is going to find it dangerous

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:53.980
to swim for 2-3km without breathing.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.430
I’ll be putting my life at stake here.

00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:00.940
Actually, I will, without a doubt, die. I’m a deadweight in water, after all...

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.990
Forgive me, Mr. Clown.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:09.030
Yeah, this is the least we can do for you.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.820
Your life is on the line, after all.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.200
Thank you, Japanese representatives.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:17.080
But, what should we do?

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:20.470
It’s impossible to make a delicious bread with such disgusting fish.

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:33.270
All right! With this,
I can buy a little time.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.770
And now, I should come up with another escape...

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:37.860
I thought of something good!

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:40.520
It’s <i>Japan</i> #22!

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.620
Did I say something bad?

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:50.560
No, not at all.

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:54.910
Of course it’s bad, you dumbass!

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:57.250
Really? Thank goodness.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:59.210
Be patient for just a little longer.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.380
I’ll make it at mach speed!

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:01.920
Yeah!

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:02.590
You really don’t have to pass the speed of sound.
Yeah!

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:04.380
You really don’t have to pass the speed of sound.

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:06.550
Ah, but...

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:10.680
Aren’t those blind cave tetras really disgusting?

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.220
Don’t you think that it’s a good idea to
put some more thought into making...

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:14.810
It’s all right.

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:19.060
I developed <i>Japan</i> #22 to taste
good, no matter what fish I use.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:21.310
Note: Kamaboko (<b>蒲鉾) is a block of processed fish paste.
It’s kamaboko <i>Japan</i>, after all.</b>

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:22.980
Kamaboko, you say?!
Note: Kamaboko (<b>蒲鉾) is a block of processed fish paste.</b>

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:27.310
Do you mean the kamaboko
you make by crushing fish meat?

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:28.820
Yep!

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.280
It’s true that kamaboko is made
from fish such as sharks and flying fish,

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:35.660
which are pretty dry and have no umami.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:38.330
Yeah, but does kamaboko go well with bread?

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.170
I’ll apply a trick to it, so no worries.

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:43.330
<i>Japan</i> #22 is definitely delicious.

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:46.140
Still... However... But...

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:48.880
What’s with you?!

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.840
Well, there are no definites in this world.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:56.060
If Azuma says it’s definitely
delicious, then it’s definitely delicious.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.020
Yeah, that’s right.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.350
Mr. Clown, don’t worry.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.440
Let’s make it right away!

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.350
This is really bad.

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:07.980
Based on past experience,

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.350
it’s almost a given that his bread will be really delicious.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:15.610
On top of that, he’s trying to make it faster than the speed of sound.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.290
At this rate,

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:21.210
I’ll be at the bottom of the lake
faster than the speed of sound!

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:24.800
I have no choice, then.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.270
Until I can come up with another way to escape,

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:33.130
I must prolong my reaction
for all eternity!

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:36.100
Prepare yourselves, Japanese representatives!

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:40.980
We should mix half of this minced fish with the dough.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:41.480
Oh?

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:42.980
I see, I see.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.110
While I may have said that...

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:49.530
When I am reacting to kamaboko,

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.110
what should I tie it up with at the end?

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:55.360
The trick is to season it strongly.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:57.120
Okay, let me do that.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:58.760
Kamaboko...

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:00.120
Bokoboko...

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:01.370
Kumamoto...

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:02.790
Kamatoto...

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.380
Nothing really quite fits.

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:10.300
I mean, a reaction is not something that you really think about, or prepare beforehand.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:11.420
It’s an expression of feeling

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:15.840
borne from your body and soul reacting naturally
to something that’s extremely delicious.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:19.510
However, my life is on the line this time.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:22.980
I have to think hard and come up with the longest reaction possible!

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.600
Kawachi, you should let it drain a for a while longer.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:27.860
Isn’t this enough?

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:29.440
Get serious, Baldy.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.280
We have lives at stake on this bread.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.440
Note: Mamagoto (<b>ままごと) means playing house.
We aren’t playing house.</b>

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.150
I know that!

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:37.160
Mamagoto?

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:39.610
Mamagoto... Mamadoko...

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:41.160
Where’s Mama?!
Note: Mamadoko (<b>ママどこ) means “where’s mama?”</b>

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.670
I think I can go with this!

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:49.340
I mean, I haven’t found my mother, after all...

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:50.960
In these desperate circumstances,

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:53.340
if I were to call out for my mother,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.550
I’m sure that I’d be able to make
them understand my need to find her.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:58.510
So, we just steam this now, right?

00:10:58.510 --> 00:10:59.550
Let me see...

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:03.310
However, the mamadoko
reaction won’t last more than a moment.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:06.570
How can I prolong it...?

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:08.560
Isn’t it a little too salty?

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:10.110
Sorry about that.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:13.730
I guess I seasoned it a little more than I should have.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:14.650
That isn’t good.

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.530
You should calculate exactly how much salt you’re going to add.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:17.570
Calculate?!

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:18.780
True, but...

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.570
It’s not like you to use a word like “calculate.”

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:23.160
Calculate?

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.330
That is it!

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:29.580
Instead of making it a simple pun,
I should make my reaction into a code!

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:32.590
While they may all be first-class bakers...

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.130
they seem to be bad at calculating things.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:36.590
So, while they figure out the answer,

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:40.590
I will have plenty of time to think about another escape route.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.570
There is a message within this numerical value.

00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:58.400
The RSA codes used in computers and on the Internet

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:03.200
are also created based on such numerical values.

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.670
All right, now we just have to bake it.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:09.210
What the...

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.210
What’s Pierrot-han up to?

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:15.010
11 times 26 is 286...

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:18.470
And... putting them all together...

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:24.100
It’s done!
It’s done!

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:25.770
Already?!

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:28.480
You really did do it at mach speed,
did you?

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:29.940
Huh?

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:33.140
I messed up, so it wasn’t that fast.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:36.480
Yeah, I think we probably took about two hours.

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:38.490
Damn it...

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:42.990
I got so involved with figuring out
the code that I lost track of the time.

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:47.450
Oh... Oh well, that’s all right.

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:52.750
Either way, it’s going to take
everyone a long time to figure out my code.

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:55.750
All right, let me sample it.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:57.970
Here you go. <i>Japan</i> #22.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:12:59.750
All right.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:01.310
Chomp.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:09.730
Delicious!

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:12.310
At this rate, my feelings will...

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:14.390
c-c-come out straight.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:16.780
Kawachi!

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:18.610
You must be strong!

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:20.320
Be strong, Pierrot!

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.900
You cannot do a regular reaction!

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:34.580
If I don’t want to drown here...

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:35.420
The code...

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:38.250
I must scream out the code!

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:42.850
104,877,443,673!!!

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:46.180
Wh-Wh-What the...? I don’t get it!

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:49.640
This is a code.

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:50.930
A code?

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:52.230
That’s right.

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:55.640
This number is based on the 26 letters of the alphabet.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:58.230
Until you figure out the meaning of this code,

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.110
I will not go into the river.

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:02.400
Ju... Just wait a moment.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.650
Why must we do something like that?

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:06.160
Yeah...

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:09.790
Isn’t it more important to get out of here quickly, than to solve that code?

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:12.660
Do you have a problem with my reaction?

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:18.710
This may be my very last reaction.

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:20.920
It’s a reaction with my life on the line.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:23.420
When you put it like that...

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:28.900
104,877,443,673.

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:31.900
Come on, what’s the meaning of this code?

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.890
With this, I can buy us some time.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:47.620
Come on. 104,877,44...

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:48.520
I got it!
Come on. 104,877,44...

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:52.380
The answer is M-a-m-a-d-o-k-o.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:54.950
“Where’s Mama.” Right?

00:14:56.960 --> 00:15:00.090
H-H-H-H-H-How did you know that?!

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:02.710
It’s really simple.

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:07.760
104,877,443,673.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.470
If you do mental arithmetic on it in base 26,

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:12.850
13 times 26 raised to the power of 7,

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:14.970
plus 1 times 26 raised to the power of 6,

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:16.850
plus 13 times 26 raised to the power of 5,

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:18.650
plus 1 times 26 raised to the power of 4,

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:20.730
plus 4 times 26 raised to the power of 3,

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:22.820
plus 15 times 26 raised to the power of 2,

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:24.980
plus 11 times 26,

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:26.370
plus 15.

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:28.290
That’s what you get.

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:33.870
Now, the numbers 13 and 1 appear twice to form m-a-m-a.

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:37.500
4, 15, 11 and 15,

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:40.960
when compared to the alphabet, give you d-o-k-o.

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:44.510
Well, that’s how we get “mamadoko.”

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:47.270
You did all that by mental arithmetic?!

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:50.510
Are you the Rainman?

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:54.020
Azuma, you had that kind of power?

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:56.230
I’m pretty surprised, too.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:16:00.440
Despite my appearance, I’ve been
strong with numbers since childhood,

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.980
but I’m terrible at learning Japanese.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:04.520
Really?

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:06.190
Then, can you solve this problem?

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:10.030
98,765 times 4321.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:14.030
It s 426,763,565!

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:18.660
Is that right, Suwabara?

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:19.620
How would I know?!

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.000
Don’t ask questions to
which you don’t know the answer!

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:25.510
He’s right...

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:30.590
This must be what they mean when they say,
“there’s a fine line between genius am idiocy.”

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:31.340
How mean...

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:36.430
The reaction I was planning to prolong for all eternity

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:41.150
ended up being so short that it was
completed within the time of the eyecatch.

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:43.110
I guess I have no choice, then,

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:46.350
but to tell them the meaning
behind “where’s Mama” for all eternity.

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:47.980
In that case,

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:53.240
I can just tell them my life story
again, all the way from the beginning.

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:55.910
That’s impressive.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:58.450
Yeah, “where’s Mama” is correct.

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:01.330
And this is where the show starts.

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:04.420
Let me explain the meaning
of “where’s Mama” in detail...

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:06.040
That isn’t necessary.

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.670
You don’t need to tell us, Mr. Clown.

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:12.180
Uh, but...

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:14.180
That’s right.

00:17:14.180 --> 00:17:16.510
You grew up without knowing your mother’s face.

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:21.520
We really understand why you’d
convert “kamaboko” to “mamadoko”

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:23.650
in the desperate circumstances
of being trapped within this cave.

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:24.980
Yeah.

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:27.310
We really don’t need any more words.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.770
I hope you meet your mom soon!

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:35.670
Guys...

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:39.990
And, for precisely that reason,
you must swim quickly.

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:40.990
Yeah, swim.

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:42.000
Swim!

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:49.710
Go on... Go on... Go on...

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:55.470
Everything backfired,

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:58.470
and I’m in the worst possible spot that I could be.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:02.780
What can a deadweight in
water like me do at such a time?

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:06.150
Oh yeah...

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:07.980
H-Hey!

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:12.480
This bread was really delicious, so before I
jump in, I would like you to tell me the recipe.

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:13.820
Okay.

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:15.740
Well, in order to help you meet your mom quickly,

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:16.990
I’ll explain it to you at mach speed.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:20.120
Listen carefully.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:23.660
First, mince the fish, wash it with water, and squeeze all of the wafer out.

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:24.750
By the way,

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:28.370
adding water to the fish and washing it helps to get rid of any unnecessary elements.

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:31.880
Next, cut the mix in half, and use one
of the halves to make a slab of kamaboko.

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:35.300
Season the other half to taste,
and knead it into the bread dough.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:39.300
Next, dice the kamaboko slab
and add it to the bread dough.

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:40.970
All that remains is letting it ferment.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:43.850
After that, you steam it, but it’s delicious if you bake it normally too.

00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:44.560
That’s it.

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:45.770
After that, you steam or bake it...

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:46.690
Oh no!

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:48.400
What am I taking notes for!

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:51.650
More importantly, it was over so fast that I couldn’t even buy any time!

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:53.860
So, that should be enough, right?

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:55.700
Let’s do our best and swim!

00:18:56.660 --> 00:18:58.280
Your mother’s waiting.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.160
Do your best.

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:01.660
Hurry up and swim.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:05.120
I guess there’s nothing more that I can do.

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:09.220
I guess I just have to suck it up and jump in.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:11.670
I don’t know where you might be,

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:13.500
but don’t leave me now...

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:15.770
Mama!

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:22.430
To think that after becoming a
world-level clown who could do anything,

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:25.890
not knowing how to swim would become a fatal flaw...

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:29.980
Since circuses don’t have pools,
clowns don’t need to swim in the first place,

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:34.990
and since I didn’t have a family, I couldn’t go on family trips to the beach, either.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:45.410
Mama!

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:47.050
Mama...

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:53.460
Oh, I would have liked to go to the beach with you, just once...

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:58.420
and after swimming for a while, play in the sand with all my heart...

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:00.720
Pierrot-chan, très bien.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.140
Pierrot, would you like to play catch?

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:05.730
Okay!

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:09.060
I’m a world-level clown,

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:11.610
so acrobatics are a piece of cake!

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:15.030
What’s wrong, Pierrot-chan?

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:17.690
Looks like you made an error in catching it.

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:20.360
That’s strange...

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:23.240
Here.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:28.250
Another error, eh?

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:30.450
Catch it this time, okay?

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:31.510
Okay.

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:36.920
Oh I see!

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:39.090
Note: This is a reference to the protagonist Hoshi Hyuuma, from the baseball anime “Kyojin no Hoshi.”
This must be third Major League...

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:40.680
Enlightenment!
Note: This is a reference to the protagonist Hoshi Hyuuma, from the baseball anime “Kyojin no Hoshi.”

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:41.980
No, he isn’t!

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:44.890
But, it’s strange...

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:49.820
Why did I make an error in
catching a regular pitch like that?

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:52.860
What is it?

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:55.030
What’s happening?

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:02.360
Huh?

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:03.790
I can breathe!

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:08.620
Since I was making errors all the time, I can breathe now!

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:10.620
I’m breathing via errors!

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:12.700
I’m breathing via era?

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:14.790
I’m breathing via era.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:19.720
I’m breathing with gills!
Note: “Error” is pronounced as “eraa” in Japanese; “era” (<b>鰓) means “gills.”</b>

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:24.220
I see!

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:26.510
The minced blind cave tetra had

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:29.770
the most muscular portion around the cheek of the fish,

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:33.060
in other words, the portion with the gills, mixed in it.

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:36.230
Be it tuna or snapper, the cheek portion is the most delicious part.

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:39.520
To think that they remembered
to add that into the kamaboko...

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.570
Thank you, Japanese representatives!

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:44.410
Thanks to your bread,

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:48.000
I’m able to swim!

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.960
We’ve been told that Pierrot and
Kazuma’s party have been untraceable.

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:02.550
I wonder if they’re really all right...

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:04.260
Well, considering the kind of people they are...

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:06.020
I’m sure that they’re alive somewhere.

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:14.890
What the...

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:17.240
My...!

00:22:22.190 --> 00:22:23.530
What is it?!

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:25.500
What in the world happened?!

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:28.950
D-Damn it.

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:32.750
I cannot breathe with gills in the air.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:08.090
Our royal palace possesses
ingredients gathered from all over the world.

00:24:08.090 --> 00:24:09.470
You can take whatever you like.

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:10.590
How generous!

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:11.170
However!

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:13.390
Only if you can answer my question.

00:24:13.390 --> 00:24:14.140
What was that?!

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:15.550
Next time, on Yakitate!! Japan,

00:24:15.550 --> 00:24:16.640
The King’s Question!!

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:19.060
Who is the first person you feed your delicious-looking bread to?

00:24:19.060 --> 00:24:19.890
Be sure to watch!

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:22.650
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:25.110
Kamaboko originated from Japan.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:27.070
Because it resembles the ear of the plant cattail,

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:29.150
it used to be called “gama no ho.”

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:31.660
The ear of a cattail, or gamahoko, eventually became kamaboko.

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:33.750
Oh?!
